Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enables efficient parallelization of challenging synthesis problems. It involves dynamically identifying which unknowns in a synthesis problem are best suited for explicit search and which are better suited for symbolic search through constraint solving. The main idea is to partially concretize a randomly chosen, but likely highly influential, subset of the unknowns to be synthesized, allowing for a combination of exponential hill climbing and binary search to find the optimal size of the concretized subset. This approach has been shown to outperform traditional synthesis methods in many cases and has good parallel scalability.
Sources: DBLP conf cav JeonQSF15 (2015); DBLP journals fmsd JeonQSF17 (2017)
