A: The main idea of the sketching approach to program synthesis is to allow programmers to create partial programs, known as sketches, which describe the desired implementation strategy while leaving low-level details unspecified. An automated synthesis procedure then fills in these details. This approach bridges the gap between high-level programming insights and the computer's ability to manage low-level implementation tasks.

The CEGIS (Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis) algorithm finds the program holes by efficiently computing the values for these holes and performing equivalence checks between the synthesized program and a reference implementation. It uses test cases, called harnesses, to specify the solution for a sketch, ensuring that the filled holes conform to the desired specifications.
