Q: What is adaptive concretization?
A: Adaptive concretization is a program synthesis technique that enhances the efficiency of parallel program synthesis by dynamically identifying which unknowns are best suited for explicit search and which are better for symbolic search through constraint solving. The method involves "partially concretizing a randomly chosen, but likely highly influential, subset of the unknowns to be synthesized." It employs a combination of exponential hill climbing and binary search to determine the optimal size of the concretized subset, using a statistical test to evaluate the effectiveness of different degrees of concretization. This approach allows for a highly parallel implementation, significantly speeding up the search process. 

In summary, adaptive concretization combines the strengths of symbolic and explicit search strategies to improve the performance of program synthesis tasks. 

References:
- [paper: Adaptive Concretization for Parallel Program Synthesis | year: 2015]
- [paper: An empirical study of adaptive concretization for parallel program synthesis | year: 2017]
