A: The Euler system is designed for solving difficult optimization problems involving programs. Specifically, it aims to find an input \( x \) to a function \( P \) (written in a standard C-like language) such that the output \( P(x) \) is minimized with respect to an appropriate distance measure on the space of outputs of \( P \). The paper states, "Find an input x to P such that the output P(x) of P is minimized." This capability is particularly useful in software engineering, where many problems can be framed as optimization questions. 

Additionally, Euler can handle program synthesis problems that reduce to numerical optimization, such as designing a controller for parallel parking a car.
